<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <RootNamespace>Charm.History</RootNamespace>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!-- ★ S89 — THE SEAM IS THIS PROJECT.

       `PersonId`, `SeasonId` and `GameId` hide a `long`. Nothing outside this
       assembly may see that long: no accessor, no cast, no comparison operator.
       The whole point is that domain code CANNOT write "person 4001 is older than
       person 4000" or "person id 12 is on school 12" — those inferences are false
       and the type system is what makes them unwritable.

       The mechanism is the assembly boundary: the raw accessors below are
       `internal`, and there is DELIBERATELY NO `InternalsVisibleTo` here. Adding
       one would silently unseal every guarantee A2 asserts. If a future session
       needs the raw value outside, it adds a method HERE and defends it, rather
       than opening the wall. -->

</Project>
